## Slot vocabulary ({{slot-count}} slots inherited or declared)

Memoir memorials inherit `:mm/Memory` plus type-specific required slots:

- **`methodology`** (required) — one-line description of the process
- **`provenance`** (required) — origin of source material + when the process occurred
- **`exclusions`** — what was deliberately NOT read / included
- **`sanitization-log`** — list of (candidate, disposition, reason) triples for anything that went through the firewall
- **`firewall-check-status`** — `passed` / `passed-after-sanitization` / `pending`

{{slot-table}}

## Canonical body structure

```markdown
## Premise

<What process was undertaken, what triggered it, what the
intended output is.  One paragraph.>

## Methodology

<The method is load-bearing.  Spell out: what was read (by scope,
not file path if proprietary), what was excluded and why, what
abstraction discipline was applied, what firewall / sanitization
gates fired, what evidence standard each finding had to clear.>

## Provenance + exclusions

<Explicit exclusion list.  The value of saying "I did NOT read X"
is nearly equal to the value of saying "I read Y and found Z" —
it bounds the memoir's epistemic reach.>

## Findings

<The actual extracted content, organized however suits the
process.  For pattern-extraction memoirs: one section per
extracted pattern, each with a citation-equivalent.>

## Sanitization log (if any firewall fired)

<Table of (token-candidate, disposition, reason) so the audit
record is complete.>

## Confidence / limitations

<What the memoir is NOT — what questions it doesn't answer, what
evidence it lacks, what should be re-visited if source access
becomes stable.>

## See also

<Cross-links to: the protocol that governed the process, patterns +
references that resulted, parent arc if any.>
```

## Audit invariants (future)

- Every `type: memoir` entry has a `methodology:` line
- Every memoir has a `provenance:` line
- Memoirs extracted from proprietary sources have `firewall-check-status: passed` (error if `pending`)
- Memoir body contains a `## Methodology` section (warning if absent)

## Pitfalls

- **Using memoir where library is correct** — if the source is publicly re-readable AND you cited specific file:line evidence, it's a `library`, not a memoir
- **Methodology-thin memoirs** — a memoir without a methodology section is just a weakly-sourced library study; methodology IS the substrate
- **Forgetting the exclusion list** — if you read 10 dirs out of 40, the 30 you DIDN'T read are load-bearing context for future sessions
- **Sanitization theater** — a sanitization log that only says *"passed"* isn't a log; record actual token-candidates that came up and what happened to each
- **Memoir rot** — memoirs age; the source may have moved, the methodology may be superseded.  Add `last-reviewed:` and update when re-read

## Composition with adjacent classes

The **protocol ↔ memoir pair** is load-bearing: a protocol memorial names the rule; a memoir memorial records what happened when the rule was applied.  Every memoir that applied a protocol should cite it; every protocol may accumulate memoirs as instances.

- **`library`** — the type memoir most resembles; distinguishing line is *"durable source + re-readable citations"* vs. *"fragile-source / process-record"*
- **`plan`** — forward-looking artifact; memoir is backward-looking
- **`decision`** — records a choice; memoir records a process
- **`log`** — sibling session-record; a single extraction session typically produces one memoir (methodology + findings) and one log (session chronicle) that cross-link
- **`protocol`** — the rule memoirs of structured extraction instantiate

